{{also|Anfängen}}
==German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|anvahen||to grab on}}, from {{m|gmh|an||on}} + {{m|gmh|vahen||to grab}}, corresponding to {{prefix|de|an|fangen}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈanˌfaŋən/|[ˈanˌfaŋən]|[ˈanˌfaŋŋ̩]}}
* {{audio|de|De-anfangen.ogg|Audio}}
* {{hyphenation|de|an|fan|gen}}
* {{audio|de|De-at-anfangen.ogg|audio (Austria)}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb|an.fangen<fängt#fing,gefangen.haben,sein[Northwest German colloquial]>}}

# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[begin]]; to [[commence]]
#: {{ux|de|Das Konzert '''fängt''' gleich '''an'''.|The concert is '''beginning''' shortly.}}
# {{lb|de|intransitive|with {{l|de|zu}} + infinitive}} to [[start]] doing something
#: {{ux|de|Wann hast du '''angefangen''' zu rauchen?|When did you '''start''' smoking?}}
# {{lb|de|intransitive|with {{l|de|mit}}}} to [[begin]] something; to [[start]] something
#: {{ux|de|Morgen '''fangen''' wir mit dem Projekt '''an'''.|Tomorrow we are '''starting''' the project.}}
# {{lb|de|intransitive|with {{l|de|von}}}} to [[start]] a topic; to keep talking about something
#: {{ux|de|Er '''fängt''' ständig von seinen Hunden '''an'''.|He’s always '''talking''' about his dogs.}}
# {{lb|de|transitive|colloquial}} to [[begin]] something; to [[start]] something
#: {{ux|de|Wann hast du das Rauchen '''angefangen'''?|When did you '''start''' smoking?}}
#: {{ux|de|Morgen '''fangen''' wir das Projekt '''an'''.|Tomorrow we are '''starting''' the project.}}
# {{lb|de|transitive|colloquial|usually with {{l|de|mit}}}} to [[put to good use]], to [[do]] something good (with)
#: {{ux|de|Haben Sie nichts Besseres mit Ihrer Zeit '''anzufangen'''?|Don't you have anything better to '''do''' with your time? }}

====Usage notes====
* The normal auxiliary for this verb is {{m|de|haben}}: {{m|de||Ich habe angefangen|t=I have begun}}. The auxiliary {{m|de|sein}} is however used by some speakers in northwestern Germany in everyday speech: {{m|de||Ich bin angefangen}}. (Many of these speakers however use the standard forms in formal contexts in speech and writing.) Compare the same construction in Dutch {{m|nl|beginnen||to begin}}: {{m|nl||Ik ben begonnen}}.<ref>[https://www.atlas-alltagssprache.de/runde-4/f01c/ Atlas zur deutschen Alltagssprache: ist/hat angefangen ]</ref>
* The construction of {{m|de|anfangen}} as a transitive verb (sense 5) is perfectly acceptable in spoken German, but would not be common in formal writing.

